 521	\begin{definition}[The combined strategy]
 522		\label{def:ld-combined-strategy}
 523		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedMeasuredQuestion}
 524		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedMeasuredDirection}
 525		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedRebaseParameter}
 526		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedAnswerMap}
 527		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedStrategy}
 528		\leanok
 529		\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate,
 530			def:tensor-product-strategy, def:bracket,
 531			def:ld-combining-parameters, def:ld-combining-map}
 532		Let $\mathcal S = (\psi, A, B)$ be a strategy for the directly indexed
 533		low-degree game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$.  The
 534		\emph{combined strategy} $\mathcal S^{\mathrm c}$ for the directly indexed
 535		low-degree game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}^{\mathrm c}$ has the same
 536		state and the same two Hilbert spaces, and answers each question of the
 537		combined game by measuring one question of $\mathcal S$ and post-processing
 538		its outcome.  Writing questions of the combined game in the coordinates of
 539		Definition~\ref{def:ld-combining-map}, the five cases are the following.
 540		\begin{enumerate}
 541			\item On the point question $(u,\alpha) \in \F_q^{m+k}$: measure the
 542			point question $u$, obtaining $b \in \F_q^k$, and answer
 543			$\sum_r \alpha_r b_r$.
 544			\item On the axis-parallel line question with stored index $i < m$ and
 545			canonical base $(u_0,\alpha_0)$: measure the axis-parallel line question
 546			with index $i$ and base $u_0$, obtaining univariate polynomials
 547			$f_0,\ldots,f_{k-1}$ of degree at most $d$, and answer
 548			$\sum_r (\alpha_0)_r f_r$, again of degree at most $d$.
 549			\item On the axis-parallel line question with stored index $m + r$ and
 550			canonical base $(u_0,\alpha_0)$, a line along which $u$ is constant and
 551			only the coordinate $\alpha_r$ varies: measure the point question $u_0$,
 552			obtaining $b$, and answer the degree-one polynomial
 553			$t \mapsto \sum_s (\alpha_0)_s b_s + t\, b_r$.
 554			\item On the diagonal line question with stored index $i < m$, canonical
 555			base $(u_0,\alpha_0)$ and direction $(v,w)$: measure the diagonal line
 556			question with index $i$, direction $v$ and the canonical base of the
 557			line $\ell(u_0, v)$, rebase the answer along the affine change of
 558			parameter relating the two presentations of that line, obtaining
 559			univariate polynomials $f_0,\ldots,f_{k-1}$ of degree at most $md$ in the
 560			parameter of the combined line, and answer
 561			$t \mapsto \sum_r \bigl((\alpha_0)_r + t\, w_r\bigr) f_r(t)$, of degree
 562			at most $md+1 \le (m+k)d$.
 563			\item On the diagonal line question with stored index $i \ge m$,
 564			canonical base $(u_0,\alpha_0)$ and direction $(0,w)$, a line along which
 565			$u$ is constant: measure the point question $u_0$, obtaining $b$, and
 566			answer $t \mapsto \sum_r (\alpha_0)_r b_r + t \sum_r w_r b_r$.
 567		\end{enumerate}
 568		For any of these five question forms, if the outcome of the measured
 569		question has a different answer form from the one specified in the
 570		corresponding case, the post-processing returns the zero answer of the form
 571		required by the combined question.  This convention makes the
 572		post-processing a total map on the common answer alphabet.
 573		For the point measurements of the original strategy, let $r$ be the map
 574		that sends a point answer to its tuple in $\F_q^k$ and sends either kind of
 575		line answer to the zero tuple.  Write
 576		\[
 577			\widehat A^{\mathsf{Point},u}_a
 578			= \sum_{x:\,r(x)=a} A^{\mathsf{Point},u}_x,
 579			\qquad
 580			\widehat B^{\mathsf{Point},u}_a
 581			= \sum_{x:\,r(x)=a} B^{\mathsf{Point},u}_x.
 582		\]
 583		These are the point measurements post-processed by
 584		\texttt{directLdPointValuesOrZero}.  In particular, each zero-tuple effect
 585		is the original zero-tuple point effect plus all wrong-form answer effects;
 586		those additional effects need not vanish.  Use the same convention for the
 587		combined strategy, with its own answer alphabet.
 588	
 589		Cases~3 and~5 use that a line of $\F_q^{m+k}$ whose direction
 590		has vanishing point part is contained in a fiber of the projection to
 591		$\F_q^m$, so that the combined polynomial restricted to it is determined by
 592		the $k$ values of the tuple at the single point $u_0$; the rebasing in
 593		case~4 is the affine change of parameter of the previous paragraph, needed
 594		because the
 595		canonical base of a line of $\F_q^{m+k}$ need not project to the canonical
 596		base of its image line in $\F_q^m$.
 597	\end{definition}
