 664	\begin{lemma}[Value of the combined strategy]
 665		\label{lem:ld-combined-value}
 666		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directLdRejectionProbability_directCombinedStrategy_le}
 667		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.directCombinedStrategy_value_ge}
 668		\leanok
 669		\uses{def:ld-combined-strategy, lem:ld-combined-question-law,
 670			def:ld-win-predicate, def:tensor-product-value}
 671		The statement holds with the universal constant $C = 10$: if $\mathcal S$
 672		succeeds with probability at least $1 - \eps$ in the directly indexed
 673		low-degree game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$, then
 674		$\mathcal S^{\mathrm c}$ succeeds with probability at least $1 - 10\eps$ in
 675		the directly indexed low-degree game with parameters
 676		$\mathsf{ldparams}^{\mathrm c}$.  The value $10$ is used in the absorption of
 677		Proposition~\ref{prop:ld-simultaneous-general-k}.
 678	\end{lemma}
 679	\begin{proof}
 680		\leanok
 681		\uses{def:ld-combined-strategy, lem:ld-combined-question-law,
 682			def:ld-win-predicate}
 683		Both games weight their nine ordered type pairs uniformly, so it suffices to
 684		bound the rejection probability of each branch of the combined game by a
 685		bounded number of branch rejection probabilities of the original game and to
 686		sum.  Fix a branch and split according to the event of
 687		Lemma~\ref{lem:ld-combined-question-law}.
 688	
 689		On the event $\mathbf{i} < m$ the two combined answers are the images of the
 690		two original answers under the relabelings of
 691		Definition~\ref{def:ld-combined-strategy}, and each clause of
 692		Definition~\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} for the combined game follows from the
 693		corresponding clause for the original game.  For the point-versus-point
 694		clause this is immediate.  For a line-versus-point clause, let $t$ present
 695		the sampled combined point on the sampled combined line; then $t$ presents
 696		the point part of the sampled point on the image line, the combining part of
 697		the sampled point is $\alpha_0 + t w$ in the diagonal case and $\alpha_0$ in
 698		the axis-parallel case, and the combined line answer evaluated at $t$ is
 699		$\sum_r (\alpha_0 + t w)_r f_r(t)$, respectively $\sum_r (\alpha_0)_r f_r(t)$;
 700		substituting the original clause $f_r(t) = b_r$ for every $r$ turns this into
 701		the combined point answer $\sum_r \alpha_r b_r$.  For a line-versus-line
 702		clause, equality of the two original tuples of line answers gives equality of
 703		their combinations.
 704	
 705		On the event $\mathbf{i} \ge m$ all measured questions are the point question
 706		at the same point $u$, and every clause of the combined game follows from the
 707		agreement $b = b'$ of the two original point answers, which is the
 708		point-versus-point clause of the original game: in cases~3 and~5 the combined
 709		line answer is a fixed affine function of the measured tuple, and evaluating
 710		it at the parameter presenting the sampled combined point returns
 711		$\sum_r \alpha_r b_r$, the combined point answer computed from the same
 712		tuple.
 713	
 714		Each branch of the combined game is therefore rejected with probability at
 715		most the sum of two branch rejection probabilities of the original game, and
 716		summing over the nine branches bounds the rejection probability of
 717		$\mathcal S^{\mathrm c}$ by an absolute multiple of that of $\mathcal S$.
 718	\end{proof}
